From the project root:

```bash
# Stop everything and delete the Postgres volume
docker compose down -v

# Start again (backend runs alembic upgrade on boot)
docker compose up -d
```

`-v` removes named volumes including `postgres_data`, so you get an empty DB. Migrations apply when `backend` starts.

If you only want to wipe Postgres (keep Redis/etc.):

```bash
docker compose stop backend worker beat
docker compose rm -f postgres
docker volume rm verspect_postgres_data   # name may be depintel_postgres_data — check with: docker volume ls | grep postgres
docker compose up -d
```

Check the exact volume name with:

```bash
docker volume ls | grep postgres
```

Also clear the browser `localStorage` tokens (`access_token` / `refresh_token`) or you’ll stay “logged in” to a user that no longer exists.